var migrateCmd = &cobra.Command{
	Use:   "migrate",
	Short: "Database migration tools",
	Long: `Database migration tools for generating and managing schema migrations.

HypGo's migration system reads your Go model structs through bun's own table
metadata (the same columns, types and constraints bun's CREATE TABLE uses),
compares them against a stored JSON snapshot, and generates up/down SQL
migration files compatible with golang-migrate.

Workflow:
  1. Define or modify your model structs with bun tags
  2. Run "hyp migrate diff" to generate SQL migration files
  3. Review and apply the generated SQL
  4. The snapshot is automatically updated after diff

Subcommands:
  diff       Compare current models with snapshot, generate SQL migrations
  snapshot   Save current model schema as a baseline snapshot

The snapshot is stored at .hyp/schema_snapshot.json by default and records
the dialect it was taken with.

Examples:
  hyp migrate diff                    Generate PostgreSQL migration
  hyp migrate diff --dialect mysql    Generate MySQL migration
  hyp migrate snapshot                Save current schema snapshot`,
}

var migrateDiffCmd = &cobra.Command{
	Use:   "diff",
	Short: "Generate migration SQL from model changes",
	Long: `Compare current Go model structs (registered via GlobalRegistry) against
the stored schema snapshot, and generate up/down SQL migration files.

Models are registered by your own code (models generated by hyp generate model /
hyp api call migrate.GlobalRegistry().Register in init()), so this command runs
the project (go run <pkg>) with HYPGO_MIGRATE_OUT and HYPGO_MIGRATE_DIALECT set:
server.Start() writes the registered schema and returns without listening.
Anything main() does before Start() (loading config, connecting to the database)
still runs.

Detected change types:
  - AddTable / DropTable          Model added / removed (removing the last model drops its table too)
  - AddColumn / DropColumn        Field added / removed
  - AlterColumn                   Type, NOT NULL, DEFAULT or autoincrement changed
  - RenameTable / RenameColumn    Only with a rename hint (see below)
  - AddUnique / DropUnique        unique / unique:<name> changed

Renames: without a hint a renamed field is a DROP COLUMN + ADD COLUMN (data loss).
Tag the field with its old column name, or the bun.BaseModel field with the old
table name:
  FullName string ` + "`bun:\"full_name\" migrate:\"renamed_from=name\"`" + `
  bun.BaseModel   ` + "`bun:\"table:members\" migrate:\"renamed_from=users\"`" + `
When a table has a dropped and an added column of the same type, diff prints a hint.

Destructive changes (DROP TABLE, DROP COLUMN, narrowing or incompatible type
changes, adding a NOT NULL column without DEFAULT to an existing table) make diff
fail without writing anything unless --allow-destructive is given.
Changes that cannot be expressed in SQL (primary key changes, adding/removing
SERIAL on PostgreSQL) also fail; the snapshot is only updated when every change
produced SQL.

Files are named <YYYYMMDDhhmmss>_<name>.up.sql / .down.sql (golang-migrate).
PostgreSQL files are wrapped in BEGIN; ... COMMIT;.

Flags:
  -d, --dialect             postgres (postgresql, pg) or mysql (mariadb); default postgres
  -o, --output              Output directory for migration files (default: migrations/)
  -s, --snapshot            Snapshot file path (default: .hyp/schema_snapshot.json)
  -p, --pkg                 Main package to run (default: .)
  -n, --name                Migration name used in the file names (default: auto)
      --allow-destructive   Allow DROP TABLE / DROP COLUMN / narrowing type changes

Exit codes: non-zero when the project cannot be run or never calls server.Start(),
when the snapshot was taken with another dialect or an older hyp, on destructive
changes without --allow-destructive, and on changes that cannot be expressed.

Examples:
  hyp migrate diff                              PostgreSQL, default paths
  hyp migrate diff --dialect mysql              MySQL dialect
  hyp migrate diff -n add_user_bio              migrations/<ts>_add_user_bio.up.sql
  hyp migrate diff -o db/migrations/ -d mysql   Custom output directory`,
	RunE:         runMigrateDiff,
	SilenceUsage: true,
}

var migrateSnapshotCmd = &cobra.Command{
	Use:   "snapshot",
	Short: "Save current schema as snapshot",
	Long: `Scan all registered models and save the current schema to the snapshot file.

This snapshot is used as the baseline for future "hyp migrate diff" operations.
Run this command to establish or reset the baseline after:
  - Initial project setup
  - Manually applying SQL changes outside of HypGo migrations
  - Resolving merge conflicts in the snapshot file
  - Upgrading from an older hyp migrate snapshot format

Column types depend on the dialect, so use the same --dialect as for diff.
The snapshot is stored as JSON at .hyp/schema_snapshot.json by default.

Like "diff", this runs the project (go run <pkg>) to read the registered models.

Flags:
  -d, --dialect    postgres (postgresql, pg) or mysql (mariadb); default postgres
  -s, --snapshot   Snapshot file path (default: .hyp/schema_snapshot.json)
  -p, --pkg        Main package to run (default: .)

Examples:
  hyp migrate snapshot
  hyp migrate snapshot -d mysql -s db/schema.json`,
	RunE:         runMigrateSnapshot,
	SilenceUsage: true,
}

